## Windows host, round 6 (`973a4080`): gate C PASSED -- and the launch bug was mine

`gate.txt`: `reached: D; result: FAIL; first failure: Gate D CDP -- Electron launch: no folder open in
workbench, Pulse composer disabled; doc's arg list format is wrong for this Electron version`, with
**gate C compile PASS**. That is the first real-build proof of the registration: `npm run compile` on the
owner's machine, whole tree, TS2459 gone.

Cause of the D failure, in my doc: I wrote `-ArgumentList '--remote-debugging-port=9222','--user-data-dir',
'D:\path'`. Chromium flags take values with `=`, so the flag was left valueless and the path became a
positional -- read as "what to load", not "the folder to open". No folder, disabled composer, and every
downstream check blaming the UI for a quoting mistake. The operator should not have to know that, so
`scripts/verify_pulse_manager_registration.mjs` now owns it: it builds correct argv (`--user-data-dir=`,
`--disable-extensions`, exactly one positional workspace, defaulting to a temp scratch folder so an open
folder is never optional), waits for the workbench page target, delegates the checks to
`validate_pulse_ui_cdp.js --manager-only`, then evaluates the row query itself and writes
`session-rows.json`. `--dry-run` prints the argv, `--no-launch` attaches to a running IDE, and the
failure paths exit 2/3/4 with the fix in the message. Verified here by dry-run and by both failure paths;
the launch itself needs Electron, which only his host has.

Two pins added, one of them after its own bug: the first version asserted the broken form was absent from
the docs and failed on the sentence that *explains* the bug. It now scans only ```powershell blocks --
every `--user-data-dir` inside a command must be followed by `=`. A pin that matches prose will match
tomorrow's prose. Lane: 12 passed, including the in-lane scoped typecheck on the restored tree.

## Windows host, round 7 (`de47fc5f`): the positional was the app, not the folder -- per code.bat

`session-rows.json` came back `rows: 0, composerDisabled: true`, which means the script's own half worked:
it built the process, waited for the CDP page target, attached over WebSocket and evaluated the row query.
The zero is a true reading of an empty workbench, not a harness fault. His diagnosis was right: this
`.build/electron/PulseAI.exe` is bare Electron, and `desktop/vscode/scripts/code.bat:48` is
`%CODE% . %DISABLE_TEST_EXTENSION% %*` -- the first positional is the app directory. Passing only a folder
made Electron `require` it: `Cannot find module 'D:\...\playground'`.

Fix, derived from the repo rather than guessed: the script now passes `desktop\vscode` first when
`.build/electron/resources/app/package.json` is absent (dev build) and omits it when present (packaged
build), then flags, then the folder as the trailing positional; `--folder-uri` carries the folder as a flag
instead, which removes positional ordering from the question entirely. A preflight over CDP now runs *before*
the harness and writes `preflight.json`; if the composer is disabled it exits 5 printing the `--folder-uri`
retry and the hand-open alternative, so "no folder" can never again be reported as a broken UI.

Pinned: the app-dir-first shape, the disk check for packaged-vs-dev, and the preflight, with the lane green
at 12 passed (including the in-lane scoped typecheck) and both argv forms verified by `--dry-run` here.

