## Live key, first real turn: 42s of model time, then a planner crash (fixed)

`hi` on a real provider produced `Thought 42s`, the honest `Turn accepted. Waiting on the model...` line, and
`Run failed: 'str' object has no attribute 'model_dump'`. Not a UI defect: three sites in
`src/graphs/chat_graph.py` projected planner steps with `step.model_dump()`, assuming a pydantic model.
Steps arrive as dicts from checkpointed state and as bare text from a provider that answers with a list of
strings, and `TaskPlan.steps` can be assigned without validation -- so the assumption belonged to the call
site, not the schema. `steps_to_dicts` now owns that boundary next to the model: text becomes a real
`{id, description, status: pending}`, an existing `status` is preserved, and the key
`start_next_plan_step` indexes unguarded always exists.

Verified here by executing the projection over all three shapes, and both directions of the text pin
(re-injecting the unsafe call made it fail, restoring made it green). The missing assistant body above the
failure is the same crash, not the removed fallback: with the turn surviving, real narration has a source.

Also on record: this sandbox reset its branch pointer to the base commit mid-round, so a fix commit landed on
`86eaaae2` without the ten commits above it. The push was rejected as non-fast-forward, which is the only
reason that did not become a mess; the work was re-applied on `18c9647f` instead of rebased blindly.

## Modes, from the fork's own wiring: why `hi` planned, and what Hermes does instead

`src/bridge/protocol.py:10` -> `EXECUTION_MODES = {agent, plan, debug, ask}`, default `agent`
(`src/bridge/__main__.py:275,512`), which is what the composer's `Agent` dropdown selects.

* `ask` -- `after_task_manager` returns `"ai"` immediately (`chat_graph.py:2160`), and every phase-guidance,
  delivery and tool branch is skipped on `execution_mode == "ask"` (`:799,818,871,896`). No planner, no
  classifier call. A greeting costs one turn.
* `plan` -- planner runs, `after_planner` routes to `plan_preview` (`:2207`): plan shown, nothing executed.
* `agent` -- planner runs, then `after_planner` -> `ai` executes. Planning is therefore ON for every
  interactive turn unless `should_create_plan` declines.
* `debug` -- same shape with debug phase handling (`:809`).

The fork already knows the cost: `chat_graph.py:2075-2078` says Pulse "previously spent multiple provider
calls classifying, generating, and validating a plan before the action loop, unlike Hermes' direct headless
conversation loop", and exempts that -- but only for autonomous workspace runs, so an interactive `hi` in
Agent mode still paid for the classifier plus a plan, then crashed in the projection (previous section).
`should_create_plan` had exactly one free bypass, a list of request verbs, and a greeting has no verb: the
gate could recognise a question but not the absence of a request.

Fix keeps mode semantics and adds a zero-call social bypass (`_looks_like_phatic_turn`): every token must be
known-phatic, drawn-out spellings collapse their whole trailing run ("okkk" -> "ok"), and it is a whitelist
rather than a length heuristic because a false skip would drop planning on real work. Executed with
`get_llm` sabotaged, so a phatic turn that reaches the model factory fails the test; verified in both
directions by deleting the gate (13 failures) and restoring it (18 pass). Lane: 20 passed.

## Hermes source read directly (`0cbc6e3`), and the pre-action planner is gone

The owner's correction was right and the upstream proves it: `tools/todo_tool.py:316` is ONE function the
model calls (`todos=None` reads, a list replaces, `merge` updates by id) with `:15` "Behavioral guidance
lives entirely in the tool schema description", and `agent/plan_prompt.py` makes PLAN MODE a prompt fed as a
normal turn -- "There is no engine and no model-tool footprint", deliverable markdown under
`.hermes/plans/`. Asking is a tool too (`tools/clarify_tool.py`). Capability in the loop, mode as a prompt,
zero cost when unused.

Pulse had the mirror image and paid for it on `hi`: classifier call, generation call, validation, then the
step projection crash. Three changes, in Hermes' shape:

* `src/tools/plan_tool.py` -- `plan_update`, bound into the graph's tool list (38 tools), schema description
  carrying the abstention rules; the state write in `progress_node` lets the model's list replace the plan
  outright, and `plan_created` is now tracked so the UI and the gates see it.
* `planner_node` -- returns `_no_plan()` for `agent`/`ask`/`debug` with the factory sabotaged in tests and
  no call happening; `plan` mode returns Hermes' prompt as a turn and generates nothing. The advisory chain
  was cut from the node rather than left unreachable, and `after_planner` sends plan mode to the agent
  instead of previewing an empty list.
* `"plan"` has no reducer, so a second planner pass now returns `{}` when a plan exists -- otherwise the
  model's own list would be erased on the next step.

Executed, not asserted by prose: 9 tests in `test_hermes_planning_parity.py` import the real graph module
(installed langgraph plus the provider SDK set into a fresh venv for exactly this) and run the node -- 9 passed.
Across the five related lanes the measured result is **37 passed, 8 skipped**, not the 45/2 first written here: the
8 skips are `pulse-webview/node_modules` missing after this sandbox rebuilt, so the esbuild/tsc-backed lanes
could not run. Recorded as a host gap, not a pass; his round-5 `npm run compile` is what covers the build.
The claim that `hi` is now one turn is provable only on his host -- that is the test.

## Plan mode painted `0 steps` in the panel -- less visible than the CLI, and now fixed

The owner asked whether plan mode shows anything in the desktop UI. It did not: `planner_node` returns
`"plan": []` for the prompt path, and the panel's Plan section reads `${model.plan.length} steps`, so a
`/plan` turn painted `0 STEPS` with a single `write_file` row as its only evidence. Hermes' CLI behaves the
same way (a number, then you open the file) and that is fine there; in a window with a plan inspector, an
artifact nobody can see is a worse plan.

Fix is a Pulse-specific `PLAN_SURFACE_CONTRACT` appended *after* the frozen corpus text -- the same local
adaptation as the `.hermes/plans` -> `.pulseai/plans` swap -- telling the agent to write the file first,
then mirror the same steps through `plan_update` once, and not to keep two lists. `PLAN_MODE_RULES` stays
byte-identical to `upstream_corpus.json`: both prompt-parity lanes pass unchanged (70 passed, 1 skipped).

## Planning parity verified on Windows (gate 5 PASS, `8bbcc3cf`), and the CopilotKit tab stops needing a server

His run: `reached: 5; result: PASS; first failure: none`, `tools.txt` = `plan_update bound: True | tools: 38`
(the same 38 this side measures), and the three planning lanes at **30 passed, 1 warning** -- zero failures,
zero skips, so no host gap on the phatic gate, the step projection or the graph node. The `UTF-16LE`
encoding of `Tee-Object` output was decoded to read the summary line; ASCII-grepping that log finds
nothing, which is worth remembering when reading his artifacts.

Then the CSP item from his console, which was ours: `renderCopilotWebview` iframed a raw
`http://localhost:5173` into the workbench document, whose `frame-src 'self' vscode-webview:` refuses any
http origin before the dev server is asked. The fork's own vendored Copilot extension has zero `<iframe>`
uses and shows every web UI through a webview -- but a webview is not required here: `'self'` already
covers files under the app root. So the SPA is now *built into the app*: `npm run build` in `pulse-webview`
emits with a relative `base`, a `postbuild` step copies `dist/` into
`.../contrib/pulseai/browser/media/pulseai-spa/` (and into `out/` when it exists), and
`pulseai.copilotWebview.url` defaults to `local`, loading it via `FileAccess.asFileUri`. No domain, no
port, no proxy, and the same code path works in a packaged build or a remote window. The per-mode notice
now tells a local-mode user to build rather than to start a server; the ignore rules keep the artifact out
of git.

Checked here: the scoped strict typecheck passes on his tip with these edits (`1 passed`), the copy script
lands a probe bundle with its relative asset paths intact and `git status` stays silent on it, and
`vite.config.ts` is syntax-clean under tsc. What only his machine can settle: the tab actually painting
after one build.

